{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer and return duty on sale of rental property","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving all notices, paying interest, and returning the deposit plus accrued earnings or interest.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental property that is sold or transferred; the new owner's duties apply whether or not the deposit and interest are transferred to the new owner.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"applies on sale or transfer of the rental property"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalty for withholding security deposits of tenants receiving public assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds the security deposit of a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance is liable for a civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 per offense; unlawfully using deposit money is a disorderly persons offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who received financial assistance through any State or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance; no court action is needed to keep participating in the program.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"tenants receiving State or federal financial or rental assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceeding under the Penalty Enforcement Law, with penalty amounts going to the State entity that made the deposits; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense punishable by a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment up to 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord may charge applicants for credit and screening reports","requirement":"A landlord may access credit reports for prospective tenants from credit bureaus or tenant screening agencies, may charge the tenant the cost of the report, and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants screened by a landlord or rental agency for a residential rental; the landlord may also perform background checks through public records and verify application information.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"prospective tenant screening for residential rentals"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Division of Housing and Community Resources, landlord-tenant publication (Credit Checks and Background Checks)","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination protection for source of lawful income","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing on the basis of the applicant's source of lawful income; source of lawful income is one of the classes the Law Against Discrimination protects.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"excludes owner-occupied two-family dwellings and rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, and age-restricted housing, as to familial status.","penalty":"Fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense (N.J.S.A. 10:5-14.1a(a)); a discriminating landlord may have to pay damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction; rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","key_value":"30 years from completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the 30-year exemption runs from completion of construction of the building. Government-subsidized rents may also be exempt from local rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings, exempt for 30 years from completion of construction"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase procedure and unconscionability standard","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent, with proper written notice. The increase must not be unconscionable or violate a municipal rent control ordinance.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; before evicting for nonpayment of an increased rent the landlord must serve a valid notice to quit and notice of rent increase.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential tenancies under the Anti-Eviction Act"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing them; a tenant may complain to a municipal rent control board where one exists.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase restriction on condominium or cooperative conversions","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; increases for new services or amenities remain allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they qualify for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Rent increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not barred.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No statewide rent control; municipalities may adopt rent control ordinances","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents; municipalities may pass rent control or rent leveling ordinances, enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rentals in the state; whether a unit is covered depends on the municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinance and its local Rent Control Board.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"statewide; coverage set by municipal rent control ordinances"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Division of Housing and Community Resources, landlord-tenant publication (Rent Control/Rent Increases)","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":"State law does not regulate rents; municipalities may regulate them by ordinance.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Publicly financed and subsidized housing exempt from municipal rent control","requirement":"HUD-owned or subsidized developments, and unsubsidized developments with HUD-insured mortgages that HUD determines have economic problems, are not subject to municipal rent control; rents fixed and controlled by the NJHMFA in projects it finances are also not subject to municipal rent control ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Housing developments owned or subsidized by HUD and HUD-insured developments HUD finds have certain economic problems; projects financed by the New Jersey Housing and Mortgage Finance Agency.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"publicly financed or subsidized housing developments"},"exemptions":"Such developments are not subject to municipal rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Division of Housing and Community Resources, landlord-tenant publication (Public Financed and Subsidized Housing)","quoted_span":"Housing developments owned or subsidized by the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD), as well as unsubsidized developments with HUD-insured mortgages determined by HUD to have certain economic problems, are not subject to municipal rent control.","interaction":"Exempts these developments from municipal rent control ordinances; rents are set by HUD or the NJHMFA instead.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process, and must have good cause and prove it in court; a Notice to Quit is required for all good cause evictions except nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; no residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential tenancies"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
